# Imputation leakage experiment — 2026-11-07

## Scope and input boundary

- Added a standalone development-only comparison of Stage 2's logistic
  regression with global versus fold-local median/mode imputation.
- Read only `stage1_outputs/X_train.csv`, `stage1_outputs/y_train.csv`, and
  `mi_stage2_baselines.py`. The experiment did not access the locked test
  partition or any test-ID data.
- The Stage 2 feature groups, target list, CV seed/fold count, and
  `build_pipeline` function are loaded from the actual Stage 2 source through
  its AST, avoiding the plotting module's temporary cache setup.
- No source data or existing Stage 2–Stage 13 outputs were modified.

## Implementation and outputs

- Added `mi_leakage_experiment.py`; it rejects missing/invalid schemas, IDs or
  outcomes in predictors, non-binary labels/features, insufficient stratified
  class counts, malformed folds, failed fits, and invalid probabilities.
- Both methods use the same materialized per-target `StratifiedKFold` splits
  (`n_splits=5`, shuffle, seed 42) and the unchanged Stage 2 logistic
  regression settings. The leakage method fits imputers on the complete
  development predictor table; the safe method fits the Stage 2 pipeline
  within each training fold. Scaling, one-hot category discovery, and logistic
  regression remain fold-local in both methods.
- Imputation leakage lets validation predictors influence global medians and
  modes; this can shift metrics in either direction.
- The output directory contains exactly:
  `aggregate_metrics.csv`, `fold_level_metrics.csv`,
  `target_split_summary.csv`, and `run_manifest.json`.
- The manifest records input paths, versions, CV/model/preprocessing settings,
  commands, output schemas, split-index digests, and captured warnings. The
  script refuses to overwrite an existing output directory.

## Commands and results

- `.venv/bin/python -B -m unittest discover -s tests -p 'test_mi_leakage_experiment.py' -v`
  — **6 tests passed**. Synthetic checks cover global imputer statistics on
  the full development predictor table (including a row with missing labels),
  training-fold-only safe imputer statistics, matching method split digests,
  metric/output schemas, invalid schemas/labels/features, and overwrite
  refusal.
- `.venv/bin/python -B mi_leakage_experiment.py` — completed all 30 fold
  evaluations (3 targets × 5 folds × 2 methods); **0 warnings** were captured.
- The aggregate rows (mean ± sample SD across folds) were:

  | Target | Imputation method | AP | AUROC | Brier |
  |---|---|---:|---:|---:|
  | DEATH_BINARY | Fold-local pipeline | 0.6507 ± 0.0306 | 0.8790 ± 0.0168 | 0.0890 ± 0.0050 |
  | DEATH_BINARY | Global before CV | 0.6420 ± 0.0237 | 0.8793 ± 0.0150 | 0.0889 ± 0.0034 |
  | ZSN | Fold-local pipeline | 0.3150 ± 0.0300 | 0.6212 ± 0.0252 | 0.1786 ± 0.0049 |
  | ZSN | Global before CV | 0.3156 ± 0.0301 | 0.6210 ± 0.0257 | 0.1785 ± 0.0050 |
  | FIBR_PREDS | Fold-local pipeline | 0.2460 ± 0.0685 | 0.7017 ± 0.0708 | 0.0888 ± 0.0077 |
  | FIBR_PREDS | Global before CV | 0.2457 ± 0.0691 | 0.7010 ± 0.0714 | 0.0890 ± 0.0078 |

- A read-only artifact check verified exact output membership, 30 fold rows,
  six aggregate rows, three target summaries, recomputed all aggregate means
  and sample SDs, matched method split digests, and confirmed no captured
  warnings and no test-partition access.
- Pylance syntax validation passed for `mi_leakage_experiment.py`; the focused
  tests also executed the test module successfully.
- Post-implementation verification: independent read-only code/output review
  returned **PASS** with no actionable findings. Re-ran the focused unittest
  command after review (6 tests passed); Pylance syntax validation again found
  no errors.
- `git diff --check` reported existing conflict markers in
  `t09_report/references.bib` (lines 2, 15, 27, 37, 39, 40, 48, 55, and 91).
  That file was already modified in the worktree before this task and was not
  changed here. The two added Python files passed a trailing-whitespace check.

